# README loadout catalog

This rule applies only in a loadout source repo (a tree with `loadouts/*.yaml`).
In a consumer project without that directory, ignore it.

When a loadout is added, removed, renamed, or its distinctive contents
change, update `README.md` **Available loadouts** in the same change.

The table must list every top-level `loadouts/*.yaml` file:

| Column | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Loadout | Exact loadout name in backticks |
| Extends | Parent loadout(s) in backticks, in YAML `extends` order, or — if none |
| What you get | Short summary of distinctive rules, skills, agents, hooks, and MCPs |

Do not ship the catalog update in a later commit or follow-up PR. Do not
leave the summary as "close enough" when a loadout's unique artifacts
changed.

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "I'll update the README later" | Later means drift. Same change. |
| "The summary is still roughly right" | Readers use this table to choose loadouts. Name what landed. |
| "It's only an internal rule/skill" | If a loadout ships it, the catalog says so. |

When you add this kind of change, also run the README catalog tests.
